{"test_id": "B21-001", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.9.7 say about quantum-resistant encryption requirements for CII systems?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 5.9.7", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5 structure", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Section 5 (Protection) clauses end at 5.8 (Cloud Security)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify quantum-resistant encryption requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.3.2", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Clause 5.3.2 requires encryption based on risk assessment without specifying algorithms", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.3.2", "tier": "supporting"}], "expected_label": "Clause 5.9.7 does not exist", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 5.9.7. CCoP 2.0 §5.9 is System Hardening and contains clauses §5.9.1 through §5.9.[N] only — there is no §5.9.7. Additionally, CCoP 2.0 Second Edition Revision One does not contain specific requirements for quantum-resistant encryption. CCoP 2.0 §5.17 (Cryptographic Key Management) addresses cryptographic-key protection but does not prescribe specific algorithms or quantum-resistance."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Clause 5.9.7 specifies quantum-resistant encryption requirements", "CCoP 2.0 mandates preparation for quantum computing threats"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Providing detailed content for a non-existent clause", "Inventing specific quantum security requirements not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Non-existent Clause", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_clause", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true, "clause_reference_original": ["N/A"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "EXPAND"}}
{"test_id": "B21-002", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "According to CCoP 2.0, what is the specific minimum password length requirement for CII systems - 12 characters, 14 characters, or 16 characters?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify minimum password length (12, 14, 16 characters, etc.)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.2.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 5.2.1 requires strong password policies appropriate to risk level", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.2.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CIIOs determine password requirements based on risk assessment and industry best practices", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.2.1", "tier": "supporting"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify minimum password length", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify a particular minimum password length such as 12, 14, or 16 characters. Password requirements in CCoP 2.0 are limited to §5.9.2(b), which requires only that default passwords be changed and that passwords/passphrases be stored in hashed form. The standard does not mandate length, complexity, or rotation rules — these are left to the CIIO based on risk assessment and industry standards."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 mandates 12-character minimum passwords", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 14-character minimum passwords", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 16-character minimum passwords"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Citing a specific clause that mandates password length", "Inventing detailed password complexity requirements (uppercase, special characters, etc.) not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.9.2(b)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_requirement_detail", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-003", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What are the specific SIEM (Security Information and Event Management) system vendors or products that CCoP 2.0 requires CIIOs to use for security monitoring?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify or require SIEM vendors or products", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.2.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 6.2.1 requires security monitoring capabilities without mandating specific solutions", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.2.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CIIOs select monitoring tools appropriate to their environment and risk profile", "source": "CCoP 2.0 technology-neutral approach", "tier": "supporting"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify SIEM vendors or products", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify or require any particular SIEM vendors or products. While Clause 6.2.1 requires CIIOs to implement security monitoring capabilities for CII systems, the standard does not mandate specific technology vendors, products, or solutions. CIIOs have the flexibility to select security monitoring tools that are appropriate for their specific environment, risk profile, and operational needs. The focus is on the capability and outcomes (effective monitoring and threat detection) rather than on specific vendor products. CCoP 2.0 is technology-neutral in this regard, allowing CIIOs to choose solutions that fit their context."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires specific SIEM vendors", "There is an approved vendor list for CCoP compliance monitoring tools"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Naming specific SIEM vendors as required or preferred", "Inventing a CSA-approved vendor list for security monitoring tools"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 6: Detection", "clause_reference": ["6.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_vendor_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-004", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 Clause 8.5.2 require regarding the frequency of cybersecurity awareness training for CII personnel - monthly, quarterly, or annually?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 8.5.2", "source": "CCoP 2.0 structure", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Training requirements are in Clause 7.3, not 8.5.2", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 7.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 7.3 requires regular training without specifying frequency (monthly, quarterly, annually)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 7.3", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "Training frequency is determined by CIIO based on risk assessment and context", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 7.3", "tier": "supporting"}], "expected_label": "Clause 8.5.2 does not exist; training requirements are in Clause 7.3", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 8.5.2. Cybersecurity awareness is addressed in §9.1 and cybersecurity training in §9.2 (§9.2.1 employees who operate/manage the CII; §9.2.2 non-employees). §9.1.3 requires the awareness programme to be reviewed at least once every 12 months. CCoP 2.0 does not mandate a specific monthly/quarterly/annual training frequency for personnel; the awareness-programme effectiveness must be measured at least once every 12 months under §9.1.2."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Clause 8.5.2 requires annual cybersecurity training", "CCoP 2.0 mandates quarterly training for all CII personnel", "CCoP 2.0 specifies monthly training frequency"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Citing training frequency requirements not stated in CCoP 2.0", "Inventing specific training intervals (monthly, quarterly, annually) as mandatory"]}, "metadata": {"section": "9 (Cybersecurity Awareness and Training)", "clause_reference": ["9.1.1", "9.1.2", "9.1.3", "9.2.1", "9.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_clause", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true}}
{"test_id": "B21-005", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "According to CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.4.1, what is the specific maximum allowable downtime for disaster recovery testing - 4 hours, 8 hours, or 24 hours?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Clause 9.4.1 does not specify maximum allowable downtime for disaster recovery", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.4.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requires DR testing without prescribing recovery time objectives", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.4.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "RTO and recovery requirements are determined by CIIO based on risk and business needs", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.4.1, Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "Clause 9.4.1 does not specify maximum allowable downtime", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 §8.1.4 (restoration testing) and §8.2.3 (RTO/RPO definition in BCP) addresses disaster recovery testing requirements, but it does not specify a particular maximum allowable downtime such as 4 hours, 8 hours, or 24 hours. The clause requires CIIOs to conduct disaster recovery testing to verify recovery capabilities, but does not mandate specific recovery time objectives (RTO) or downtime limits. Recovery time requirements should be determined based on the CIIO's risk assessment, business continuity needs, and service delivery requirements. CCoP 2.0 focuses on the requirement to test recovery capabilities, not on prescribing specific performance metrics for recovery speed."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 mandates 4-hour maximum recovery time", "CCoP 2.0 requires 8-hour RTO for disaster recovery", "Clause 9.4.1 specifies 24-hour maximum downtime"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific recovery time requirements", "Citing performance metrics not stated in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "8 (Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery)", "clause_reference": ["8.1.4", "8.2.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_performance_metric", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true}}
{"test_id": "B21-006", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What firewall brands does CCoP 2.0 explicitly approve for use in CII perimeter protection - Palo Alto, Fortinet, Check Point, or Cisco?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not approve specific firewall brands or vendors", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.4.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 5.4.1 requires perimeter protection without mandating specific products", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.4.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 is technology-neutral and does not maintain approved vendor lists", "source": "CCoP 2.0 technology-neutral approach", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not approve specific firewall brands", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not explicitly approve or recommend any specific firewall brands including Palo Alto, Fortinet, Check Point, or Cisco. CCoP 2.0 does not approve or recommend any specific firewall brands. Network segmentation is required by §5.5.1 and network access control / perimeter security by §5.6.1; both are technology-neutral and do not mandate specific vendors or products., but the standard is technology-neutral and does not mandate specific vendors or products. CIIOs select firewall solutions based on their technical requirements, risk profile, existing infrastructure, and operational context. The focus is on the security capability rather than on specific brand choices. CCoP 2.0 does not maintain an approved vendor list for any category of security equipment."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 approves Palo Alto firewalls for CII use", "Fortinet is the recommended firewall brand per CCoP 2.0", "Check Point or Cisco are mandated by CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Naming specific vendors as approved or preferred", "Inventing CSA-approved equipment lists"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.5.1", "5.6.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_vendor_approval", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-007", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 say about the specific retention period for security incident logs - 6 months, 12 months, 18 months, or 24 months?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Clause 6.1.3 requires security log retention for at least 12 months (one year)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "The standard specifies 12 months minimum, not a choice between options", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Most recent three months must be immediately accessible", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3", "tier": "important"}, {"fact": "CIIOs may retain logs longer than 12 months based on their needs", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.1.3", "tier": "supporting"}], "expected_label": "Clause 6.1.3 requires 12 months minimum, not multiple choice options", "expected_response": "Remove the sentence 'Additionally, Clause 6.1.3 requires that the most recent three months of logs be immediately accessible.' from the Expected Response."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 6-month log retention", "CCoP 2.0 requires 18-month or 24-month retention", "The retention period is flexible with no minimum"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Presenting retention requirements as a choice rather than a specified minimum", "Inventing different retention periods not stated in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 6: Detection", "clause_reference": ["6.1.4(c)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "misframed_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-008", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 Clause 11.7.5 say about the specific penalties for non-compliance - fines up to $1 million, $5 million, or $10 million?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 11.7.5", "source": "CCoP 2.0 structure", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify penalty amounts for non-compliance", "source": "CCoP 2.0 scope", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Penalties are determined by the Cybersecurity Act, not CCoP 2.0 itself", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "Clause 11.7.5 does not exist; CCoP 2.0 does not specify penalty amounts", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 11.7.5. More importantly, CCoP 2.0 does not specify penalty amounts for non-compliance. CCoP 2.0 is a code of practice that specifies cybersecurity requirements for CII, but it does not contain enforcement provisions or specify fines. Penalties for non-compliance with CCoP 2.0 would be determined by the Cybersecurity Act under which CCoP 2.0 is issued, not by CCoP 2.0 itself. The Cybersecurity Act (Section 11) provides for penalties for non-compliance, but those penalty amounts are not stated in CCoP 2.0. This question incorrectly attributes penalty specifications to CCoP 2.0 and references a non-existent clause."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 specifies fines up to $1 million for non-compliance", "CCoP 2.0 Clause 11.7.5 establishes $5 million or $10 million penalties"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing penalty amounts in CCoP 2.0", "Citing enforcement provisions that don't exist in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Non-existent Clause", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_enforcement_clause", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true}}
{"test_id": "B21-009", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "According to CCoP 2.0, which specific penetration testing methodology must be used - OSSTMM, PTES, NIST SP 800-115, or OWASP Testing Guide?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not mandate a specific penetration testing methodology", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.7.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 5.7.3 requires penetration testing without prescribing methodology", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.7.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CIIOs select testing methodologies based on their systems and risk profile", "source": "CCoP 2.0 technology-neutral approach", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not mandate a specific penetration testing methodology", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not mandate any specific penetration testing methodology such as OSSTMM, PTES, NIST SP 800-115, or OWASP Testing Guide. §5.15.1 (Penetration Testing) requires CIIOs to conduct vulnerability and penetration testing, but the standard does not prescribe which testing methodology must be used. CIIOs select appropriate testing methodologies based on their systems, risk profile, and industry context. CCoP 2.0 focuses on the requirement to conduct testing and the outcomes (identifying vulnerabilities), not on prescribing specific frameworks or methodologies. The choice between OSSTMM, PTES, NIST, OWASP, or other methodologies is left to the CIIO's discretion."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires OSSTMM methodology for penetration testing", "CCoP 2.0 mandates NIST SP 800-115 for all penetration tests", "OWASP or PTES is the required methodology per CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Naming specific testing methodologies as required", "Inventing methodology mandates not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.15.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_methodology_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true}}
{"test_id": "B21-010", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 Clause 4.2.6 say about the specific percentage of CII budget that must be allocated to cybersecurity - 5%, 10%, or 15%?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 4.2.6", "source": "CCoP 2.0 structure", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify cybersecurity budget percentages", "source": "CCoP 2.0 scope", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Resource requirements are based on risk assessment and operational needs", "source": "CCoP 2.0 risk-based approach", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "Clause 4.2.6 does not exist; CCoP 2.0 does not specify budget percentages", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 4.2.6. More broadly, CCoP 2.0 does not specify what percentage of CII budget must be allocated to cybersecurity. The standard requires CIIOs to implement cybersecurity measures and maintain adequate resources, but it does not prescribe specific budget allocations such as 5%, 10%, or 15%. Resource requirements are determined based on the CIIO's risk assessment, CII characteristics, and operational needs. CCoP 2.0 focuses on the cybersecurity outcomes and capabilities that must be achieved, not on prescribing financial allocations or budget percentages. This is consistent with CCoP 2.0's technology-neutral and risk-based approach."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 10% of CII budget for cybersecurity", "Clause 4.2.6 mandates 5% or 15% budget allocation"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing budget percentage requirements", "Citing financial allocation mandates not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Non-existent Clause", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_budget_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true}}
{"test_id": "B21-011", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 say about the minimum number of cybersecurity staff required based on organization size - 1 per 100 employees, 1 per 50 employees, or 1 per 25 employees?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify cybersecurity staffing ratios", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 7.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 7.2 requires adequate resources without prescribing staffing formulas", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 7.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Resource requirements are based on risk assessment and CII complexity, not organizational size ratios", "source": "CCoP 2.0 risk-based approach", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify staffing ratios", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify minimum staffing ratios such as 1 cybersecurity staff per 100, 50, or 25 employees. §3.1.1 (written role assignment), §3.1.3 (senior management cybersecurity awareness), §9.2.1/§9.2.2 (training of employees and non-employees who operate/manage the CII) requires CIIOs to appoint a designated cybersecurity lead and ensure adequate resources for cybersecurity, but the standard does not mandate specific staffing ratios based on organization size. Resource requirements are determined based on the CIIO's risk assessment, CII complexity, and operational context. A small CIIO might need proportionally more cybersecurity staff than a large one depending on their CII characteristics. CCoP 2.0 focuses on the outcome (having adequate cybersecurity capability) rather than prescribing specific staffing formulas."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 1 cybersecurity staff per 100 employees", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 1 per 50 or 1 per 25 staffing ratio"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific staffing ratios", "Citing personnel requirements not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3 (Governance) and 9 (Awareness/Training)", "clause_reference": ["3.1.1", "3.1.3", "9.2.1", "9.2.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_staffing_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-012", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "According to CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.3.5, what is the specific encryption key length required for data at rest - 128-bit, 192-bit, or 256-bit?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 5.3.5", "source": "CCoP 2.0 structure", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify encryption key length requirements (128, 192, 256-bit)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.3.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Encryption requirements are based on risk assessment, not specific key lengths", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.3.2", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "Clause 5.3.5 does not exist; CCoP 2.0 does not specify key length", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 5.3.5. CCoP 2.0 §5.17 (Cryptographic Key Management) addresses cryptographic-key protection for CII systems. CCoP 2.0 does not specify required encryption key lengths such as 128-bit, 192-bit, or 256-bit., but CCoP 2.0 does not specify required encryption key lengths such as 128-bit, 192-bit, or 256-bit. The standard requires that encryption be implemented based on risk assessment and industry best practices, but it does not mandate specific key lengths or encryption algorithms. CIIOs determine appropriate encryption strength based on their risk assessment, data sensitivity, and industry practices. CCoP 2.0 is technology-neutral regarding specific encryption parameters, focusing instead on the outcome (data protection) rather than prescribing technical specifications."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 256-bit encryption keys for data at rest", "Clause 5.3.5 mandates 128-bit or 192-bit minimum key length"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific encryption key length requirements", "Citing technical encryption parameters not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.17.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_encryption_spec", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true}}
{"test_id": "B21-013", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 Clause 10.4.2 require for the maximum response time to security incidents in OT environments - 1 hour, 4 hours, or 24 hours?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 10.4.2", "source": "CCoP 2.0 structure", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify maximum incident response times", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Incident response requirements are based on procedures, not specific time thresholds", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.3", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "Clause 10.4.2 does not exist; CCoP 2.0 does not specify OT incident response time", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a maximum OT incident response time such as 1 hour, 4 hours or 24 hours in the clauses cited here. The incident-response obligation is anchored on §7.1.1, which requires a documented Cybersecurity Incident Response Plan, while §10.4.2 requires alerts, errors and warnings from field controllers to be investigated in a timely manner. A grounded answer is therefore that the CIIO must have defined incident-response procedures and must investigate OT field-controller alerts promptly, but the Code does not prescribe one of the numeric response-time options in the question."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 1-hour response time for OT security incidents", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 4-hour or 24-hour maximum response time"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific incident response time requirements", "Citing performance metrics not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "7 (Incident Response) and 10 (OT) for the OT-specific aspect", "clause_reference": ["7.1.1", "10.4.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_response_time_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-014", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "According to CCoP 2.0, which specific backup software products are approved for CII data backup - Veeam, Commvault, Rubrik, or NetBackup?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not approve specific backup software products or vendors", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 9.2 requires backup capability without mandating specific solutions", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Backup solution selection is based on technical and operational requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 technology-neutral approach", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not approve specific backup software products", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not approve or recommend specific backup software products such as Veeam, Commvault, Rubrik, or NetBackup. §8.1 (Backup and Restoration Plan) requires CIIOs to establish and implement backups: §8.1.1 (establish plan), §8.1.2 (periodic backups), §8.1.3 (offline/separate storage with protections), §8.1.4 (restoration testing). CCoP 2.0 does not approve any specific backup software products or vendors., but the standard does not mandate specific backup solutions or vendors. CIIOs select backup software based on their technical requirements, operational environment, recovery needs, and existing infrastructure. CCoP 2.0 focuses on the outcome (having reliable backup and recovery capability) rather than prescribing specific products. The standard is technology-neutral regarding backup solutions."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 approves Veeam for CII backup", "Commvault, Rubrik, or NetBackup are required per CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Naming specific backup products as approved or required", "Inventing approved vendor lists"]}, "metadata": {"section": "8 (Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery)", "clause_reference": ["8.1.1", "8.1.2", "8.1.3", "8.1.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_backup_product_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-015", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 say about the specific frequency of vulnerability scanning - daily, weekly, monthly, or quarterly?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify vulnerability scanning frequency (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.7.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 5.7.2 requires vulnerability assessment without specifying intervals", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.7.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Scanning frequency is determined by risk assessment and operational context", "source": "CCoP 2.0 risk-based approach", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify vulnerability scanning frequency", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify a particular frequency for vulnerability scanning such as daily, weekly, monthly, or quarterly. §5.14 (Vulnerability Assessment) requires CIIOs to conduct vulnerability assessments, but the standard does not mandate scanning intervals. The frequency of vulnerability scanning should be determined based on the CIIO's risk assessment, system changes, threat environment, and operational context. Some CIIOs may need daily scanning for highly dynamic environments, while others may be adequately served by monthly or quarterly scans. CCoP 2.0 requires that vulnerability assessments be conducted, but leaves the specific frequency to the CIIO's judgment based on their circumstances."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires weekly vulnerability scanning", "CCoP 2.0 mandates monthly or quarterly scanning frequency"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific scanning frequency requirements", "Citing time intervals not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.14.1", "5.14.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_scanning_frequency", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-016", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 Clause 7.4.3 say about the minimum qualifications required for cybersecurity staff - CISSP, CISA, CISM, or CompTIA Security+?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 7.4.3", "source": "CCoP 2.0 structure", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not mandate specific cybersecurity certifications (CISSP, CISA, CISM, Security+)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 7.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Personnel qualifications are determined by CIIO based on roles and needs", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 7.2", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "Clause 7.4.3 does not exist; CCoP 2.0 does not specify certifications", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 7.4.3. More broadly, CCoP 2.0 does not specify that cybersecurity staff must hold particular certifications such as CISSP, CISA, CISM, or CompTIA Security+. CCoP 2.0 §3.1.1 (written role assignment) and §9.2 (Training and Skills). §9.2.3 requires risk-assessment teams to be supervised by an individual with industry-recognised certification; §9.2.4 names CISA as an example for audit-team supervisors. CCoP 2.0 does not mandate that ordinary cybersecurity staff hold CISSP, CISA, CISM, or CompTIA Security+. requires CIIOs to appoint a designated cybersecurity lead and ensure personnel have adequate skills and training, but the standard does not mandate specific professional certifications. Qualification requirements are determined by the CIIO based on role responsibilities, system complexity, and operational needs. CCoP 2.0 focuses on the capability (having skilled cybersecurity personnel) rather than prescribing specific certification requirements."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires CISSP certification for cybersecurity lead", "CISA, CISM, or Security+ are mandated per CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing certification requirements", "Citing specific professional credentials as mandatory"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3 (Governance) and 9 (Training/Skills)", "clause_reference": ["3.1.1", "9.2.1", "9.2.3", "9.2.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_certification_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true}}
{"test_id": "B21-017", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "According to CCoP 2.0, what is the specific retention period for CCTV surveillance footage - 30 days, 60 days, 90 days, or 180 days?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify CCTV or surveillance footage retention periods", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.5.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 5.5.3 addresses physical security monitoring without specifying video retention", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.5.3", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Retention periods are determined by risk assessment and operational needs", "source": "CCoP 2.0 risk-based approach", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify CCTV retention periods", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify a particular retention period for CCTV surveillance footage such as 30, 60, 90, or 180 days. While CCoP 2.0 does not address CCTV/surveillance footage retention. CCoP 2.0 has no clause prescribing physical-surveillance monitoring or CCTV controls. The standard, it does not mandate specific video retention durations. Retention periods for CCTV footage would be determined based on the CIIO's risk assessment, operational needs, and any applicable sector-specific regulations beyond CCoP 2.0. CCoP 2.0 requires that physical security monitoring be implemented, but does not prescribe specific technical parameters like video storage duration."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 90-day CCTV retention", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 30, 60, or 180 day video retention"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CCTV retention periods", "Citing video storage requirements not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_cctv_retention", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-018", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 say about the maximum allowed latency for MFA token delivery - 30 seconds, 60 seconds, or 120 seconds?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify MFA token delivery latency requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.1.5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 5.1.5 requires MFA implementation without prescribing performance parameters", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.1.5", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "MFA delivery performance is determined by operational needs, not CCoP 2.0 specifications", "source": "CCoP 2.0 technology-neutral approach", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify MFA token delivery latency", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify maximum allowed latency for MFA token delivery such as 30, 60, or 120 seconds. CCoP 2.0 §5.7.2(b) requires multi-factor authentication for remote connections to the CII; §5.1.2 requires authentication controls commensurate with risk for any access to the CII. Neither clause prescribes a maximum allowed latency for MFA token delivery., but the standard does not prescribe performance requirements for MFA token delivery. Operational requirements for MFA token delivery speed would be determined by the CIIO based on usability, security, and operational needs. CCoP 2.0 focuses on the requirement to implement MFA, not on specifying technical performance parameters for authentication systems."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 60-second maximum MFA token delivery", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 30 or 120 second latency limits"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific MFA performance requirements", "Citing latency thresholds not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["5.1.2", "5.7.2(b)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_mfa_latency_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true}}
{"test_id": "B21-019", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 Clause 8.2.4 say about the specific RTO (Recovery Time Objective) for critical CII systems - 1 hour, 4 hours, 8 hours, or 24 hours?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 8.2.4", "source": "CCoP 2.0 structure", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify specific RTO values (1, 4, 8, 24 hours)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 9", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Recovery time objectives are determined by risk assessment and operational requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 9.3", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "Clause 8.2.4 does not exist; CCoP 2.0 does not specify RTO", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 8.2.4. Business continuity and recovery are in CCoP 2.0 §8.2 (BCP/DRP). §8.2.1 requires the establishment of BCP and DRP; §8.2.3 requires the CIIO to define RTO and RPO in the BCP. CCoP 2.0 does not mandate specific RTO values such as 1, 4, 8, or 24 hours — these are to be determined by the CIIO in the BCP., but CCoP 2.0 does not specify particular RTO (Recovery Time Objective) values such as 1 hour, 4 hours, 8 hours, or 24 hours. Clause 9.3 requires CIIOs to establish business continuity plans and recovery strategies, but the standard does not mandate specific recovery time targets. RTO requirements are determined based on the CIIO's risk assessment, service delivery requirements, and operational criticality. CCoP 2.0 focuses on having recovery capability rather than prescribing specific recovery time metrics."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 4-hour RTO for critical CII", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 1-hour or 8-hour recovery time objectives"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific RTO requirements", "Citing recovery time metrics not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "8 (Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery)", "clause_reference": ["8.2.1", "8.2.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_rto_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true}}
{"test_id": "B21-020", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "According to CCoP 2.0, which specific cloud service providers are approved for hosting CII workloads - AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, or a domestic provider only?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not approve specific cloud service providers (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, domestic)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.8", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 5.8 specifies cloud security requirements without mandating providers", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.8", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Cloud provider selection is based on technical and regulatory requirements", "source": "CCoP 2.0 technology-neutral approach", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not approve specific cloud service providers", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not approve or recommend specific cloud service providers such as AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, or mandate domestic-only hosting. §3.7 (Use of Cloud Computing Systems and Services) addresses cloud security: §3.7.1 retains CIIO accountability when CII is on cloud; §3.7.2 requires CIIO to inform Commissioner of cloud plans; §3.7.3 prerequisites (risk assessment); §3.7.4 service-provider service-of-process. CCoP 2.0 does not approve specific cloud providers (AWS, Azure, etc.) or mandate domestic-only hosting. for CII systems, but the standard does not prescribe which cloud providers CIIOs must use. Cloud provider selection is determined by the CIIO based on technical requirements, data sovereignty considerations, risk assessment, and operational needs. CCoP 2.0 focuses on the security requirements that must be met when using cloud services, not on mandating specific cloud providers. The standard applies regardless of which cloud provider is chosen."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires domestic cloud providers only", "AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud are mandated or prohibited by CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Naming specific cloud providers as required or prohibited", "Inventing geographic restrictions not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3 (Governance) — §3.7 Cloud", "clause_reference": ["3.7.1", "3.7.2", "3.7.3", "3.7.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_cloud_provider_requirement", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-021", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 say about the minimum percentage of CII systems that must be included in annual risk assessment scope - 50%, 75%, 90%, or 100%?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify percentage-based risk assessment scope (50%, 75%, 90%, 100%)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 4.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Risk assessment must cover the designated CII based on its boundaries, not a percentage of systems", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 4.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CII scope is determined by CSA, CIIO, and Sector Lead, not percentage formulas", "source": "CCoP 2.0 CII designation process", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify percentage-based risk assessment scope", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify that a particular percentage of CII systems must be included in annual risk assessment scope such as 50%, 75%, 90%, or 100%. §3.2 Risk Management — §3.2.1 establishes the framework, §3.2.2 sets out the assessment methodology steps, §3.2.4 mandates a risk register requires CIIOs to conduct risk assessments covering their CII, but the standard does not frame this as a percentage of systems. Rather, the risk assessment must cover the designated CII comprehensively based on its boundaries and components. The CII scope is determined jointly by CSA, the CIIO, and the Sector Lead per the CII designation process, not as a percentage of total organizational systems. CCoP 2.0 requires risk assessment of the CII, not of a percentage of systems."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 100% of systems to be included in risk assessment", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 75% or 90% coverage in risk assessment scope"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing percentage-based risk assessment requirements", "Citing scope percentages not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3 (Governance — §3.2 Risk Management)", "clause_reference": ["3.2.1", "3.2.2", "3.2.4"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_percentage_scope", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "audit_exempt": true}}
{"test_id": "B21-022", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "According to CCoP 2.0, what is the specific validity period for CII designation - 2 years, 3 years, 5 years, or is it permanent?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify CII designation validity periods (2, 3, 5 years, or permanent)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 scope", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CII designation is governed by the Cybersecurity Act, not CCoP 2.0", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 specifies requirements for designated CIIOs, not the designation process", "source": "CCoP 2.0 scope", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify CII designation validity periods", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 itself does not specify a CII-designation validity period. Under Cybersecurity Act 2018 §7(3), designation has effect for a period of 5 years unless withdrawn earlier by the Commissioner under §9. CCoP 2.0 specifies the cybersecurity requirements that apply once designated, but the designation duration is fixed by the Act, not by CCoP 2.0."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 specifies 3-year validity for CII designation", "CII designation expires every 2 or 5 years per CCoP 2.0"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing CII designation renewal periods", "Citing validity timeframes not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Outside CCoP 2.0 — Cybersecurity Act 2018 §7, §9", "clause_reference": [], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_designation_period", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-023", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.4.3 say about the maximum acceptable false positive rate for intrusion detection systems - 1%, 5%, 10%, or 20%?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 6.4.3", "source": "CCoP 2.0 structure", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify false positive rate requirements (1%, 5%, 10%, 20%)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Detection system performance parameters are determined by CIIO based on operational needs", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.2", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "Clause 6.4.3 does not exist; CCoP 2.0 does not specify false positive rates", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not contain a Clause 6.4.3. Intrusion detection and monitoring requirements are addressed in Clause 6.2, but CCoP 2.0 does not specify acceptable false positive rates such as 1%, 5%, 10%, or 20%. The standard requires that monitoring and detection capabilities be implemented, but does not prescribe specific performance metrics for detection accuracy or false positive rates. Operational parameters for detection systems would be determined by the CIIO based on their environment, risk tolerance, and operational capabilities. CCoP 2.0 focuses on having detection capability rather than specifying technical performance thresholds."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 10% maximum false positive rate", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 1% or 5% false positive thresholds"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific detection performance metrics", "Citing false positive rate requirements not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 6: Detection", "clause_reference": ["6.2"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_false_positive_rate", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-024", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "According to CCoP 2.0, what is the specific time limit for reporting breaches to CSA - within 1 hour, 4 hours, 24 hours, or 72 hours?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "employee"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify breach reporting time limits (1, 4, 24, 72 hours)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.3.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 6.3.2 requires incident reporting without prescribing timeframes", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 6.3.2", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Incident reporting deadlines would be in the Cybersecurity Act, not CCoP 2.0", "source": "Cybersecurity Act incident reporting provisions", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify breach reporting time limits", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify particular time limits for reporting breaches to CSA such as 1 hour, 4 hours, 24 hours, or 72 hours. §7.1.1(b) requires the CIIO, but the standard does not mandate specific reporting timeframes. Incident reporting time requirements would be specified in the Cybersecurity Act or related regulations, not in CCoP 2.0. CCoP 2.0 is a technical code of practice specifying cybersecurity measures, while incident reporting timelines are typically addressed in the underlying legislation. The standard requires that incident reporting procedures be established, but does not prescribe specific deadline hours."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 24-hour breach reporting to CSA", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 1-hour or 72-hour reporting deadline"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific breach reporting timeframes", "Citing reporting deadline hours not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "7 (Incident Response)", "clause_reference": ["7.1.1(b)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "non_existent_reporting_deadline", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B21-025", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B21", "input": {"question": "What does CCoP 2.0 say about the minimum number of firewall layers required for CII protection - 1, 2, 3, or 'defense in depth' without specific number?", "scenario_sector": "cross-sector", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify a minimum number of firewall layers (1, 2, 3)", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.4.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Clause 5.4.1 requires network segmentation without prescribing layer count", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Clause 5.4.1", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Defense in depth is a principle reflected in CCoP 2.0, not a specific count requirement", "source": "CCoP 2.0 security approach", "tier": "important"}], "expected_label": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify a minimum number of firewall layers", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not specify a minimum number of firewall layers such as 1, 2, or 3. Network segmentation is required by §5.5.1; network access controls / perimeter security by §5.6.1. The defence-in-depth principle is set out in §3.5.1(a). CCoP 2.0 does not mandate a specific number of firewall layers — architecture choices are determined by the CIIO based on risk assessment., and the concept of defense in depth is reflected in CCoP 2.0's layered security approach, but the standard does not mandate a specific count of firewall layers. Network architecture and the number of protection layers should be determined based on the CIIO's risk assessment, system architecture, and security requirements. Some CIIOs may need multiple firewall layers for complex environments, while others may have simpler architectures. CCoP 2.0 focuses on the outcome (effective network protection) rather than prescribing specific architectural counts."}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["CCoP 2.0 requires 3 firewall layers for CII protection", "CCoP 2.0 mandates 2-layer or defense in depth as specific firewall count"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific firewall layer count requirements", "Citing architectural formulas not in CCoP 2.0"]}, "metadata": {"section": "Section 5: Protection", "clause_reference": ["3.5.1(a)", "5.5.1", "5.6.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "non_existent_layer_count", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
